WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.110
- [Narrator] Singapore generated
around 16.3 billion pounds

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:06.183
of solid waste in 2022.

00:00:07.950 --> 00:00:10.293
Almost half of that is dumped here,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:13.023
Singapore's only landfill.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:18.420
And a portion of Semakau
Landfill is already 90% full.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.470
- Based on our estimate

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:22.500
the island will be
completely filled by 2035.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:24.510
- [Narrator] Singapore
smaller than the size

00:00:24.510 --> 00:00:28.170
of New York City is also one
of the most dense countries

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:29.220
in the world.

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:33.690
So, it's tackling its trash
by turning all of this,

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:37.783
into this in order to prolong
the life of the landfill.

00:00:37.783 --> 00:00:40.440
- I mean, where can we
form another landfill?

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:41.273
- [Narrator] The current key

00:00:41.273 --> 00:00:43.896
to Singapore's trash
problem is incineration.

00:00:43.896 --> 00:00:45.690
(fire whooshing)

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:50.370
While 55% of all waste is
recycled according to the NEA,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.340
42% goes to the waste to energy plants

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:55.410
where it's burned leaving only ash

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.560
and 3% of solid waste
to go to the landfill.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.750
For just one incineration site,

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:02.760
trucks collect around 6 million pounds

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:04.623
of municipal solid waste a day.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.987
After weighing it gets dropped off here.

00:01:07.987 --> 00:01:09.706
(glass smashing)

00:01:09.706 --> 00:01:11.700
- Okay, this is our reception hall.

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:14.190
We have one to 15 numbers gate.

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.420
- [Narrator] According to Singapore's,

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:17.280
National Environment Agency,

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.280
42% of trash collected in 2021,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.310
went to waste to energy
plants like this one.

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.880
There are four active waste
to energy plants in Singapore.

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.920
What can be processed at the facility,

00:01:28.920 --> 00:01:31.080
moves onto the waste bunker.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:32.613
At the crane control room,

00:01:33.630 --> 00:01:36.630
operators lift massive
amounts of the mixed waste

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:38.940
and feed it into these incinerators,

00:01:38.940 --> 00:01:43.080
which reach temperatures of
about 1800 degrees Fahrenheit.

00:01:43.080 --> 00:01:45.120
All of the heat from
the waste incineration,

00:01:45.120 --> 00:01:47.040
creates steam in the boilers,

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.140
which is then converted into electricity,

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.720
through these steam turbine generators.

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:55.320
This can generate an export
about 120 megawatts per hour,

00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:59.130
which can meet the demand
of about 240,000 households.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:01.500
- The TuasOne waste-to-energy plant

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:06.500
is one of the highest
electricity generation facility.

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:07.830
- [Narrator] The resulting flue gas

00:02:07.830 --> 00:02:09.960
is passed through a
catalytic fabric filter

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:11.283
to remove particles.

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:13.560
According to the EPA,

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:15.390
these incinerator tend to generate,

00:02:15.390 --> 00:02:18.660
slightly more greenhouse gas
emissions than natural gas

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:20.790
for the amount of electricity produced

00:02:20.790 --> 00:02:22.830
but less than Kohler oil.

00:02:22.830 --> 00:02:26.970
- Then the clean gas
go through the ID fan.

00:02:26.970 --> 00:02:30.600
Our stacked height is 120 meter.

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.950
With online analyzer, it
is continuously monitored.

00:02:34.950 --> 00:02:37.590
We maintain the emission
limit within the limit.

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.653
- [Narrator] Another
byproduct of the process, ash.

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.170
Lots of it.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:44.670
According to the NEA,

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:46.080
the amount of ash left

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:49.530
is roughly 10% of the
original volume of the waste.

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.300
After scrap metal is removed,

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:53.580
the remainder is transported
to the next point

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:54.573
of the process.

00:02:56.130 --> 00:02:59.760
- The ashes are loaded at
Tuas Marine Transfer Station,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.070
all the way here takes about four hours.

00:03:02.070 --> 00:03:04.020
- [Narrator] Semakau
is located five miles,

00:03:04.020 --> 00:03:05.340
south from Singapore.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:07.440
- Our batch is a huge barge.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:09.600
It's more than 85 meter long.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.270
It has a cargo of about 3,500 meter

00:03:12.270 --> 00:03:14.910
which is equivalent to one
and a half swing pool size.

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:15.870
- [Narrator] In one day,

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:19.290
about 4 million pounds of ash
and non-inconsiderable waste

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:20.700
is transferred here,

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:23.370
loaded into dump trucks
and brought to these cells

00:03:23.370 --> 00:03:24.630
for final disposal.

00:03:24.630 --> 00:03:27.180
This is considered phase
two of the landfill,

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.710
which is still a lagoon,

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:30.120
but phase one.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.010
- This is the phase one
of the landfill cell.

00:03:32.010 --> 00:03:34.020
About 90% of this landfill cell

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.030
has already completely been filled.

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:36.990
As you can see here,

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.750
mostly contains the
incineration bottom ash

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.630
and also the non-inconsiderable
waste that we receive

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.100
from the mainland Singapore.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:46.440
- [Narrator] To ensure the
landfill does not contaminate

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:47.640
the surrounding environment,

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.830
there are several measures in place

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:52.020
- Over time the landfilling and rains,

00:03:52.020 --> 00:03:54.390
will increase the water
level inside the phase two.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:56.280
So, if the water level increases,

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.500
we need to pump some of
the water out to the sea.

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.660
Before the waters are
pumped out to the sea,

00:04:00.660 --> 00:04:03.080
they are being treated by the
wastewater treatment plant.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:04.650
- Semakau perimeter bond

00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:07.200
is also lined with an impermeable membrane

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.230
that prevents leachate from
getting into the outside sea

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.960
- Leachate is like, take
it as a coffee powder.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:15.090
When you have a filter,
you have a coffee powder,

00:04:15.090 --> 00:04:16.380
you put the water over it

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:18.043
and over time this water
will start to leak out.

00:04:18.043 --> 00:04:20.580
Instead of coffee powder you have here,

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.530
it's actually the incinerated bottom ash

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.630
and the water is what you call a leachate.

00:04:25.530 --> 00:04:29.490
- Semakau Landfills Phase
One was commissioned in 1999.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.460
It's phase two area commissioned in 2015

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:34.950
is currently about 10% filled.

00:04:34.950 --> 00:04:35.910
- In the '60s,

00:04:35.910 --> 00:04:37.890
before they build waste to energy plants,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:39.540
the waste actually go to Lorong Halus

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:40.373
and then they dump this.

00:04:40.373 --> 00:04:42.240
They go there, they just throw it there

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.110
and just bury it.

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:44.910
So, currently we don't have such things,

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.850
because all domestic
waste goes to what call,

00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:49.590
waste-to-energy plants, which you visited.

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:53.010
So, what they receive from
a big amount of waste,

00:04:53.010 --> 00:04:55.598
reduced to ash, that's what comes here.

00:04:55.598 --> 00:04:56.431
(upbeat music)

00:04:56.431 --> 00:04:58.560
- [Narrator] Incinerations
plants have become more popular

00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.790
in Europe and Asia,

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.340
especially in areas with limited space.

00:05:02.340 --> 00:05:04.590
But these plants actually
decreased in number

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:06.000
in the United States

00:05:06.000 --> 00:05:10.860
from a hundred and three in
1999 to just over 70 in 2020.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.930
Critics are still
concerned about pollutants

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.320
in the incineration process
without proper equipment

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.590
and note that it's only
a short-term solution.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:23.820
With the landfill projected to
be completely filled by 2035,

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:27.090
Singapore has only
delayed the trash crisis.

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.530
The country is looking for ways

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:32.310
to recycle incinerated bottom
ash into other materials,

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.410
reducing the amount brought to Semakau.

00:05:34.410 --> 00:05:36.410
- We're working with local
universities to looking

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.610
if it's a possibility to recycle this ash

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.460
into new materials,
let's say for roads work.

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:42.810
- [Narrator] The government also hopes

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:46.260
to increase recycling
rates to 70% by 2030,

00:05:46.260 --> 00:05:48.360
which requires consumer education.

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:50.970
Currently, about 40% of items placed

00:05:50.970 --> 00:05:53.580
in recycling bins cannot
actually be recycled,

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:55.290
due to contamination.

00:05:55.290 --> 00:05:57.240
- As part of this focus,

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:59.127
we actually plans to work together

00:05:59.127 --> 00:06:00.630
in the areas such as the 3Rs,

00:06:00.630 --> 00:06:03.629
which is to reduce,
reuse, as well as recycle.

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:06.296
(upbeat music)

